WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.530
- [Instructor] When Christopher Columbus

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.420
first arrived in the Americas,

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.900
he had no way of knowing that he had

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.220
set off a complex chain of events

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.440
that would lead to everything

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.000
from humanity's largest
demographic disaster,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.780
to the founding of a new
nation nearly 300 years later,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.800
to you watching a video about the history

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.530
of that nation right at this very moment.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.890
It's strange, right?

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.400
Columbus's voyages to the New World

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.000
and those of the navigators and
settlers who came after him,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.330
changed the worlds to an extent

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.930
rarely seen in human history.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.510
Let's briefly review the effects

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.420
of these transatlantic voyages on Europe,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.420
Africa, and the Americas
between 1492 and 1607,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.700
the period between first contact

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.840
and the establishment
of the first permanent

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.910
English settlement in the
Americas at Jamestown.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.640
So you might call this the pre-British era

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.740
of colonization in the Americas.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.470
So what changes did these voyages cause?

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.860
Well, there were demographic changes,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.940
and by demographic I mean shifts

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.370
in the structure of populations.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.990
The indigenous populations of the Americas

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.970
faced a catastrophic decline in numbers

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.160
as a result of diseases
carried by Europeans.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.570
On the flip side, the
populations of Africa and Europe

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.670
grew with the introduction
of nutritious foods

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.730
found in the Americas
like potatoes and corn.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.880
Contact also led to the
creation of new cultural groups,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.270
as indigenous people,
Europeans, and Africans

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.880
mixed in the New World
and had children together.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.660
The transatlantic voyages
also led to economic changes,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:44.080
including shifts in the
systems of money and labor.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.050
Spain extracted gold and silver
from the New World by force,

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.720
compelling indigenous
people and then later

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.680
enslaved Africans to labor in mines.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.810
These riches flooded into Europe

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.150
and caused a huge increase in prices,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.960
which may have led to the development

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.080
of banking and capitalism there.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:06.730
And European demand for
laborers in New World mines

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.680
and plantations led to the
expansion of the slave trade,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.340
resulting in the forced migration

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.150
of millions of African
people to the Americas.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.910
Columbus's discovery also fostered

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.330
political changes in
Europe and the New World,

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.960
as other European countries wanted

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.590
to claim colonies of their own.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.560
And the power structure
among indigenous nations

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.320
changed, too, with the
addition of European allies

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.500
and the destruction of large
portions of the population,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.140
leading to the downfall of
powerful empires like the Aztecs.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.540
The cultures of indigenous people changed,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.340
as Europeans introduced new material goods

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.440
and animals into their environment.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.410
For example, the Spanish
brought horses to North America,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.700
changing the society of plains people,

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.610
who could now hunt and follow
herds of bison on horseback.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.330
And new religious practices
also emerged in the Americas,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.520
as indigenous peoples
combined their own beliefs

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.830
with those of Catholicism.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.140
By examining the effects
of transatlantic voyages,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.010
what we're really doing is practicing

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.120
the skill of causation.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.810
This is probably the most important

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.760
skill in the historian's toolbox.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.510
Understanding and arguing about

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.780
what caused an event to happen,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.120
or what consequences an event
had on society later on.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.330
Now this seems pretty straightforward.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.650
For example, a cause and
effect statement might be,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.690
I slept through my alarm this
morning, so I missed the bus.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.690
Cause: oversleeping.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.040
Effect: missed bus.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.690
Notice that the effect has
to happen after the cause.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.220
Missing the bus didn't
cause me to oversleep.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.430
So until we invent the time machine,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.320
effects must happen later than causes.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.460
But there can be a few
pitfalls to watch out for

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.340
when we try to determine
causes and effects.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.240
First, not everything has
just one cause and one effect.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.920
For example, maybe I
slept through my alarm

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.440
because I stayed up too
late last night watching TV.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.050
That's another cause for missing the bus.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.340
And there could be more than one effect

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.980
of sleeping through the alarm,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.750
not just missing the bus,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.530
but also missing my test in first period.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.780
Which was the most important
cause of missing the bus,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.470
oversleeping or watching too much TV?

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.000
And which was the more important effect

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.450
of sleeping through my alarm,

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.220
missing the bus or missing the test?

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.370
These kinds of questions are
what historians argue about.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.460
Which was the most important
cause of Atlantic exploration,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.890
competition between European countries,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.340
or the desire to convert
new people to Catholicism?

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.420
Another potential pitfall
is one you might have seen

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.180
in a science class or in statistics,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.530
which is correlation
does not imply causation.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.310
In other words, two events
aren't necessarily related

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.480
just because they happened
close together in time or space.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.320
For example, although epidemic
diseases led to the deaths

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.050
of indigenous people in the New World

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:10.230
at the same time that the
discovery of new foodstuffs

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.800
increased the population
of Europe and Africa,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.390
the introduction of diseases didn't cause

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.450
those populations to increase.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.900
Those were just two things
that happened simultaneously.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.500
One way you can check whether one event

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.520
was caused by an earlier one,

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.450
is to ask whether event
B would have happened

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.880
whether or not event A happened.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.010
The populations of Europe and Africa

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.590
would likely have
increased with the addition

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.550
of those new foods even if there

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.250
hadn't been any epidemic diseases at all.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.720
The last thing that we
wanna take into account

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.640
while we're thinking about causation,

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.630
is the difference between short

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:50.110
and long-term effects of an event.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.220
Missing my first period test might

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.510
be a short-term effect of oversleeping,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.710
but failing the class
because I missed tests

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.870
would be a long-term effect.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.570
And maybe because I failed that test

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:04.750
I ended up focusing on a
different subject in school,

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:06.620
and heading into a different career.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.950
That would be a really long-term effect.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:12.140
Think about the effects
of transatlantic voyages

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:13.570
that we've discussed,

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.290
and see if you can categorize them

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.520
into short-term effects
and long-term effects.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.650
You might find that some
things fit into both categories

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.330
as processes that started immediately

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.930
but then went on for a long time,

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.750
like the epidemic
diseases that immediately

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.430
affected indigenous people,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.030
but then continued to reshape
the demographic composition

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.770
of the Americas for hundreds of years.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.770
So let's recap what we've
learned about causation.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.870
First, historians argue about causation

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.820
as they try to pin down the
reason an event happened.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.110
Second, causes must happen before effects.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.200
Third, a single event might
have more than one cause

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.840
or more than one effect,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.100
and some causes and effects may be

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.930
more important than others.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:05.710
Fourth, events that
happened near to each other

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:09.640
in time or space, aren't
necessarily related

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.970
by a cause and effect relationship.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.280
Coincidences happen.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.290
And lastly, causes may have both short

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.730
and long-term effects, immediate results,

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.410
and downstream impacts that go

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.553
far beyond the time in question.

